"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,610\nThe Learning Brain. Series 8: Assessment That Teaches\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,391\nEpisode 5\n\n3\n00:00:07,471 --> 00:00:09,539\nFeedback that works\n\n4\n00:00:12,339 --> 00:00:16,356\nFeedback is often called one of the most powerful tools in teaching.\n\n5\n00:00:16,775 --> 00:00:18,429\nBut is all feedback helpful?\n\n6\n00:00:18,734 --> 00:00:20,582\nThe answer is a clear no.\n\n7\n00:00:20,752 --> 00:00:28,208\nIn 1996, Avraham Kluger and Angelo DeNisi reviewed more than one hundred thirty studies of feedback.\n\n8\n00:00:28,732 --> 00:00:30,845\nOn average, feedback helped.\n\n9\n00:00:31,261 --> 00:00:36,083\nBut about a third of the effects were negative: performance got worse after feedback.\n\n10\n00:00:36,602 --> 00:00:41,908\nFeedback was most likely to backfire when it drew attention to the person rather than the task.\n\n11\n00:00:42,078 --> 00:00:45,819\nA 2020 meta-analysis by Benedikt Wisniewski,\n\n12\n00:00:46,189 --> 00:00:52,993\nKlaus Zierer and John Hattie pooled four hundred thirty five studies with more than sixty one thousand participants.\n\n13\n00:00:53,487 --> 00:00:55,287\nThe average effect was medium,\n\n14\n00:00:55,566 --> 00:00:57,441\nabout zero point five,\n\n15\n00:00:57,727 --> 00:00:59,422\nbut it varied enormously.\n\n16\n00:00:59,592 --> 00:01:01,706\nWhat made the difference was information.\n\n17\n00:01:02,106 --> 00:01:04,584\nFeedback that told students how to improve,\n\n18\n00:01:04,604 --> 00:01:06,404\nand on what, worked best.\n\n19\n00:01:06,856 --> 00:01:10,215\nPraise alone, rewards and punishments did little.\n\n20\n00:01:10,385 --> 00:01:12,037\nIt helps to think in levels.\n\n21\n00:01:12,357 --> 00:01:15,529\nFeedback on the task says what is right or wrong.\n\n22\n00:01:15,889 --> 00:01:19,003\nFeedback on the process says how to approach it better.\n\n23\n00:01:19,366 --> 00:01:21,012\nAnd feedback about the person,\n\n24\n00:01:21,032 --> 00:01:22,309\nlike you're so smart,\n\n25\n00:01:22,383 --> 00:01:23,773\ntends to do the least.\n\n26\n00:01:23,943 --> 00:01:26,604\nThis fits wise feedback from Series five: high standards,\n\n27\n00:01:27,067 --> 00:01:30,272\nbelief that the student can meet them,\n\n28\n00:01:30,306 --> 00:01:32,033\nand specific next steps.\n\n29\n00:01:32,203 --> 00:01:32,949\nFor teaching.\n\n30\n00:01:33,399 --> 00:01:36,687\nGive fewer comments, but make them specific and actionable: one thing to keep,\n\n31\n00:01:36,909 --> 00:01:37,943\none thing to change.\n\n32\n00:01:38,068 --> 00:01:39,535\nFocus on the work and the strategy,\n\n33\n00:01:39,868 --> 00:01:42,824\nnot the person.\n\n34\n00:01:43,166 --> 00:01:45,506\nAnd build in a chance to use the feedback,\n\n35\n00:01:45,526 --> 00:01:47,833\nlike a revision or the next assignment.\n\n36\n00:01:48,373 --> 00:01:52,337\nOne tip to share with students: look for the next step in feedback,\n\n37\n00:01:52,359 --> 00:01:53,387\nnot the score.\n\n38\n00:01:53,557 --> 00:01:55,704\nFeedback works when it can be used.\n\n39\n00:01:55,924 --> 00:01:57,695\nMake every comment usable.\n\n"